<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astarte</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ashtart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Astarte was a major goddess of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, worshipped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, Canaanites and others as a deity of love, fertility and war. Known in Phoenician as Ashtart and related to the Mesopotamian Ishtar, she had great temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and Kition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified her with Aphrodite. Her cult spread with Phoenician colonies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astarte.jpg" alt="Astarte" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ismoon 21:17, 4 January 2022 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astarte_-_7th_cent_AD_-_Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_de_Sevilla-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
